Perhaps do as they did in D&D 3e -- 'bane' is a property on an item.
The D&D weapon quality can be split into two pieces.  One gives a bonus
against the type of monster, the second gives bonus damage against that
type of monster.

So, proposal:

. add the ability to add a bane quality to weapons
. a weapon may have only a single bane quality; adding a new one
  overrides and replaces the old one
. the bane might be atomic (all or nothing), or in two parts
. possibly do it using semi-alchemy:

  #dip into a 'silver potion' to get werebane
  #dip into a 'cloudy potion' to get ghostbane[1]
  #dip into a potion of gain ability to get ogre/giantbane[2]
  #dip into a potion of extra (full?) healing to for trollbane[3]
  #dip into a 'smoky potion' to get demonbane

. BUC of items affects BUC of bane weapon:

  . any cursed, item is cursed
  . both blessed, item is blessed
  . one B, one C: uncursed
  . one B, one U: maybe blessed, maybe uncursed

. you could make it maybe work (as with the summoning ghost/genie), but
  I think it'd be better to make it automatic.  The bane effect isn't
  *that* great, these potions are generally harder to get than holy
  water, and some of them are throwing away happy benefits (such as the
  potential wish from a smoky potion).

[1] I don't know offhand if it exists; bonuses apply to undead
[2] 'strength potion', and consider ogres and a type of weak giant for
    this purpose (as D&D does, in fact)
[3] the closest I can think of for regeneration


Another possibility would be to merge magic items, possibly through
polymorph.  For instance, pile weapon, ?oEnchWeapon, "oLS or =oRegen and
zap with polymorph to merge the behavior.  Suitably obscure, but it
should work automatically, I think.  Burning up a "oLS is painful (to
me, at least -- I don't mind using up =oRegen because I find it's
usually not worth the hunger).


I prefer the potion option, though.


